\f0\b\fs24 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Principles of Correct Play:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Bidding\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Before the opening lead, the hand must be at least 9/10 suitable for fulfilling the intended contract, because only modest support can usually be expected from the dummy. It is fundamentally important to know what types of cards are needed to win a given contract, so we will first review this topic.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Parti (Pt)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 The available score can be calculated as follows.\
a/ 	The marriages in the hand are sure 20 and 40 points respectively, unless due to forgetfulness or tactical reasons we ignore their announcement.\
Half of the K O pairs missing from our cards will be held by one opponent., e.g. if we have neither king nor ober of two side suits, they will probably announce 20 against us.\
\
b/ 	In a suit headed by the Ace and Ten (A-10), the cards worth 10 points\'97the major honors\'97can generally be considered certain winners. While there is a risk that one or even both of these valuable cards could be ruffed in a side suit of A-10-x or longer, this can be prevented by drawing the opponents' trumps in time.\
If we hold a singleton Ace in a suit, we can also capture the Ten with a ruff. In a suit of A-x or longer, however, capturing the Ten is only likely to succeed in exceptional cases\'97specifically, if a defender holds a singleton Ten, or if the Right-Hand Opponent (RHO) leads into our A-K suit from any combination, or the Left-Hand Opponent (LHO) leads from under the Ten.\
In a suit lacking the Ace, the Ten usually takes a trick if accompanied by the King or Ober, or if the suit is at least four cards long and one holds at least moderate trump strength. With side suits containing only two or three small cards (e.g., X x or X x x), the odds of winning a trick are around 50%, whereas a singleton Ten offers no realistic chance of scoring points. If a side suit in the combined hand (original hand plus the talon) contains two small cards and the Ten is played, the Ace will usually be captured; this is because the opponent holding the Ace will likely not play it to win the trick but will save it to capture the Ten, allowing us to trump the Ace later.\
In suits of at least two cards that lack both the Ace and the Ten\'97so-called "headless" suits\'97the high cards will belong to the opponents; with a singleton, there is roughly a two-thirds probability of scoring 10 points. If one holds no cards at all in a suit, one might expect to score 20 points, though if the hand is relatively weak and likely to concede many tricks, it is safer to count on 10 points.\
Under the rules, any high card (Ace or Ten) found in the talon always counts toward the opponents' score.\
\
c/ 	To take the last trick, you need four strong trumps\'97or at least five\'97and a hand capable of winning the majority of the tricks.\
Let\'92s assume we are playing a game of Acorns with these cards:\
		Acorns		A K U IX\
		Leaves		A X O\
		Bells		K O\
 		Hearts		O\
We can declare 20: the Acorns are worth 10, the Leaves are expected to yield 20, the Bells 0, and the Hearts either 10 or 0; the last trick also appears winnable, making for a total of 60\'9670 points. Since the opponents cannot possibly hold 20 or 40 points, the hand can likely be won through play.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Forty-One Hundred (40\'96100)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 At least four trumps\'97or exceptionally three strong ones\'97are absolutely essential, along with the 40-point declaration. Let us evaluate the high cards and the chances of taking the last trick based on the principles outlined for the hand. We should also consider the suits in which we expect to take tricks; ideally, these should be shorter than four cards in length, because if an opponent wins a trick in a suit where we are short, their partner is more likely to be forced to discard a valuable card.\
If our estimate suggests the opponents cannot score more than 30 points, this bid can be made.\
Here is an example of a well-founded 40\'96100!\
		Acorns		-\
		Leaves		X K\
		Bells		A K O U IX\
		Hearts		A X K\
It is best to begin play with a trump lower than the Ace; the outstanding trumps are likely not held by a single hand, so the opponents will not capture another valuable card with the Ten. After regaining the lead, one should draw trumps; by leading the King of Leaves, the Ten can be promoted, and the declarer takes all subsequent tricks. Even if an opponent holds three trumps, one need not concede more than 30 points.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Ultimo (Ut)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 A fundamental requirement is a trump suit of sufficient length and strength, with the seven as the lowest card. Additionally, a four- or five-card side suit\'97a "driving suit"\'97is usually necessary; by leading this suit repeatedly, one can draw out the opponents' trumps. A three-card suit can also serve as a driving suit if one or two more cards of that suit are present in the talon. Trick-taking power is also crucial; the declarer can only control the game if capable of winning the majority of tricks. The following types of hands are suitable for declaring Ulti:\
\
1./ 	A trump suit of at least six cards. With this, the bid can be made without any further conditions. Although Ulti might occasionally fail with six weak trumps and other suits lacking trick-taking potential, this risk can be mitigated by discarding in a way that leaves at least one card of every suit in hand.\
2./ 	A five-card trump suit and two or more sure high cards (an Ace or a Ten backed by at least the King/Ober). The specific suits of these high cards do not essentially matter, though with marginal overall hand strength, it is advantageous to avoid having a void in any suit. Ulti can be declared with five trumps from a hand such as this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 		
\f1\b0 Acorns		K O\
		Leaves		O U\
		Bells		A\
		Hearts		X O U VIII VII
\f0\b \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 3./ 	With a four-card trump suit and a four-card running suit. At most two high cards may be missing from the trump and running suits combined, but the two missing cards cannot both be aces. Holding a four-card running suit and a four-suited hand is preferable to holding a five-card side suit and a void. In short suits, a 1-1 split is better than 2-0. If the declarer holds the typical 4 trumps and 4 running cards, the probability of a favorable distribution\'97where the stronger defender holds no more than 5 cards total across the two suits\'97is approximately 83%. A good hand is, for example,\
		Acorns		X K U VII\
		Leaves		A U IX VIII\
		Bells		U\
		Hearts		A\
Sometimes a four-card Ultimo can be bid without a running suit, but this requires strong trumps and at least one sure trick in every suit.\
4./ 	Three trumps, exceptional hand strength. Trump holdings of A-X-VII or A-K-VII,\
an Ace-led running suit, and at least A or X-O combinations in the short suits are required.\
If forced to play from an A-X holding, part with the Ace; if the defenders attack this suit and we win with the 10, they will assume we hold the Ace and switch suits, avoiding the shortening of our trump suit.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Twenty-One Hundred (20-100)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 It is worth attempting this contract if one holds a 20-point sequence in a side suit and\'97barring extreme cases\'97does not need to lose more than 10 points. With a six-card trump suit, a high honor (Major card) is required; with a seven-card suit, the Ace is essential;\
and there must be no more than two losing tricks.\
We can bid 20-100 with these hands:\
		Acorns		A X U IX VIII\
		Leaves		X K O VIII\
		Bells		-\
		Hearts		A\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Betli (Bt)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 An ideal *betli* hand contains at most one high card lower than an Ace, while the remaining cards are sevens or belong to a sequence starting with a seven, as in this case:\
		Acorns		IX VIII VII\
		Leaves		K\
		Bells		A X IX VIII VII\
		Hearts		VII\
A Betli is unbeatable even when, despite holding high cards, they all belong to a seven-card suit missing the 8 or any higher card. A flawless Betli must be declared "exposed" (played face-up). It is also correct to undertake an exposed Betli with a single low card missing, especially with one of the following suit combinations:\
		\'96 VIII;\
		\'96 IX VIII;\
		\'96 IX VII;\
		\'96 X VIII VII.\
If the VII or VIII is missing, a suit longer than two cards is unfavorable, as there is a high risk of having to take a trick with a small card held by an opponent after the high cards have been played.\
One might bid Rebetli with two missing small cards, or even Betli with three, provided the chances of success are deemed sufficient. Key factors to consider include:\

\f2 \uc0\u9658 
\f1  It is more favorable if the missing cards are distributed across multiple suits rather than concentrated in a single one; for instance, holding two suits where the VIII is missing is preferable to holding a single suit missing the IX.\

\f2 \uc0\u9658 
\f1  A short suit with a gap is better than a longer one; for example, it is easier to avoid taking a trick with a holding of X-VII than with U-X-VII or, worse, O-U-X-VII.\

\f2 \uc0\u9658 
\f1  A hand missing a suit entirely is more advantageous than one containing all four suits.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Durchmars (Dm)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Ideally, all ten cards are either winners or belong to a suit that is guaranteed to establish\'97meaning that once the high cards are played, the lower ones will take tricks regardless of the distribution. Examples of such exceptional card combinations include the following (the first can be played in Bells, the second without trumps):\
		Acorns		A X K\
		Leaves 	-\
		Bells		A X O U IX VIII\
		Hearts		A\
\
\
		Acorns		A K\
		Leaves		A K O U\
		Bells		A\
		Hearts		A K O\
If the opponents cannot trump, we should declare an open durchmars. An open durchmars is viable even with a hand where drawing the long suit is not certain but highly probable; examples include A-X-K-x in a trump contract or A-K-O-x in a no-trump contract. With A-X-x-x-x or A-K-x-x-x, an open durchmars is riskier but still declarable, provided the opponents have not exchanged cards from the talon and\'97in the case of a trump-bearing honor\'97the combination is not in the trump suit. \
We can attempt a "cheaper" durchmars with slightly weaker suits\'97such as A-X-O-(x), A-X-x-(x), A-K-U-(x), or A-K-x-(x)\'97or even with a singleton lower than the Ace, especially if that singleton is a Ten (or a King in a no-trump game). Similarly, the pairs A-K, A-O, or A-O, A-U are acceptable in a 9-trick hand, as it is hardest for opponents to signal their holdings against high cards. The suit with the uncertain draw should not be the trump suit, so as to avoid a certain, doubled defeat in the event of an unfavorable distribution. If also undertaking an ultimo, one should not declare a durchmars with a singleton or A-x; the obligation to play toward the ultimo means the weak card must be forced out by the ninth trick at the latest, thereby reducing the chances of squeezing the defenders. Between the 6-point and 12-point versions of the no-trump durchmars, the choice should be based on an assessment of the probability of success.\
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Bidding Tactics\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 If the 10 cards in your hand\'97based on the criteria discussed so far\'97are suitable for playing a contract of higher value than the current bid, or if only one card of the appropriate suit is needed to make it viable, pick up the talon. Based on the 12 cards now held, you must select the best possible contract; this may turn out to be of higher value than the one you originally intended to play when you picked up the talon.\
When discarding, two obvious considerations must be kept in mind: retain the cards best suited for fulfilling your contract, and avoid discarding cards that could help an opponent achieve a higher-value bid. Sometimes both principles can be applied simultaneously, but often you must decide which takes precedence. Here are a few practical tips:\

\f2 \uc0\u9658 
\f1  If you have committed only to a minor suit or hearts Parti (Pt), focus primarily on not helping the opponent who might steal the bid. Discard medium-strength cards\'97especially Unters or Nines\'97from your strong or long suits; however, only discard from hearts if that suit remains strong even after the discard. Do not break up your Marriage sequences (King-Ober pairs) during the discard. If no one steals the bid after a simple Parti and you have no realistic chance of fulfilling the contract, withdraw from playing the hand.\

\f2 \uc0\u9658 
\f1  At the 40\'96100 level and above, our own performance objectives take precedence, provided we have a realistic chance of winning. Among the discard options that are otherwise neutral for our own hand, choose the one likely to be most disadvantageous to the "robber" player; for instance, when holding a sequence for a Betli (Null) contract, discard a low card, whereas for a Durchmars (Grand Slam), discard a high card. Greater caution is required if the bid is worth no more than 5\'966 points and we are void in hearts or hold only one or two low cards in it. Always monitor the others' bids, as they reveal a great deal; for example, a player who initially bid a standard Betli or Durchmars will often bid again to switch to a higher-scoring variant of the contract.\

\f2 \uc0\u9658 
\f1  Discarding is aimed solely at the success of our own contract when the risk of an opponent "stealing" the bid (robbing) is low. Common scenarios for this include: bidding after having "robbed" the hand ourselves, receiving the talon back unchanged from another player, or undertaking a contract worth at least 20 points. Of course, an opponent playing a waiting game\'97or one holding a strong hand even without the talon\'97might still step in, but this is a risk worth taking.\
If the hand, once augmented by the talon, contains 11 or 12 winning cards, the best move is to discard the ten of the strong suit, as this is the card the opponents are least likely to be able to utilize (naturally, when undertaking a 40\'96100 contract, one must not discard two tens, as that would result in a lost hand). \

\f2 \uc0\u9658 
\f1  If the unusable talon leaves no chance of winning, the sole aim of the discard is to ensure that any potential "robber" player receives the worst possible cards; therefore, one should discard two\
medium-value cards from one's strong suits. A common dilemma for the "robber" player is whether to declare their intended game immediately after discarding or\'97hoping\
for another chance to exchange cards\'97to bid only one level higher.\
The recommended course of action is as follows:\

\f2 \uc0\u9658 
\f1  With a relatively weak hand, bid without jumping levels and discard cards that the next "robber" is unlikely to find useful (e.g., one or two cards from the intended trump suit or the suit\
intended for forcing play). If no one wants the talon, take it back, discard your two worst cards, and declare the game with the best chance of success.\

\f2 \uc0\u9658 
\f1  If winning 40-100, Ultimo, or 20-100 is likely, yet there is no prospect of a more valuable contract, one should declare the bid immediately (by "jumping" to that level). It is better to prevent opponents from exchanging cards with each other to form, for example, a dangerously long trump suit or a void.\

\f2 \uc0\u9658 
\f1  With a hand that justifies a valuable contract\'97and has the potential to strengthen further (e.g., a 40-100 or higher-level Betli or Durchmars might develop alongside the Ultimo)\'97a "wait-and-see" approach (bidding without jumping) is advisable, as multiple exchanges with the talon might yield the necessary high-value cards. Of course, it is not worth waiting for the trump seven, ace, or high hearts; however, it is worth waiting for a card that fits into a suit where one is strong or completes a sequence starting with the seven.\

\f2 \uc0\u9658 
\f1  Rational play generally dictates that bidding should conclude at the level of the contract most likely to succeed. However, tactical considerations may justify deviating from this principle in two ways:\
1./ 	Against opponents who are prone to doubling (kontra) rather than playing a Rebetli, Redurchmars, Open Betli, or Open Durchmars, it is worth playing a bid one level lower. If even just one opponent doubles, successfully completing a redoubled contract yields a better payoff than winning the more expensive bid without a double. For instance, a Rebetli is worth 20 points in total, whereas a standard Betli, if redoubled, is worth 25 or 40 points. \
2./ 	Sometimes it pays to undermine another player's certain (or seemingly certain) declaration with a complex bid that is partly doomed to fail. Weighing the potential points to be won or lost, it may be rational to declare an Ultimo without the Seven, a 40-100 or 20-100 lacking the corresponding marriange, or even a hopeless Durchmarsch.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Declarations\
Choosing the trump suit\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Naming the trump suit is generally straightforward, as the decision must be made during the bidding phase, and the suit of the marriage or the Seven is often the deciding factor from the start. Occasionally, however, one must choose the trump suit from two long suits of nearly equal strength.\
For instance, with the hand below, one could play a *40-100* in either Acorns or Leaves:\
		Acorns		A K O IX\
		Leaves		X K O U\
		Bells		-\
		Hearts		A X\
In such a situation, if hearts is one of the potential choices, play in hearts, provided it does not entail excessive risk. Between two minor suits, generally designate the stronger one as trump; however, in the case of an Ultimo contract, choose the weaker of two four-card suits\'97one headed by an Ace and one without an Ace. The suit containing the Ace is preferable as a driving suit, because it allows you to force the opponents to ruff sooner.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Declaring Marriages\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 As a general rule, the declarer should declare all their marriages. With a mediocre or weak hand, declaring can help win the game, while with a stronger hand, it can help reach a score of one hundred. Defenders also generally act correctly by declaring their valuable\
card pairs. However, the defender sitting after the declarer (RHO)\'97 if holding only a 20-point marriage and a very weak hand (with no chance of taking a trick with trumps or capturing a 10-point card)\'97 may exceptionally choose not to declare, to avoid prompting the\
other defender to double without sufficient grounds.\
A player doubling an Ultimo contract might strategically withhold declaring their 40-point marriage or a 20-point marriage in the driving suit to bait the declarer into redoubling (reasoning that the doubler cannot possibly hold all the missing cards in the trump or driving suits);\
one can then "sub-double" (redouble) with a sure hand. Even if the declarer ultimately wins the game, the defenders can achieve a significant gain in this rare situation. \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \
Doubling and higher levels of doubling\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 When doubling the hand, the following can be considered certain points:\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  Marriages declared against the declarer,\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  10-point cards taken in the first trick,\
\pard\tx20\tx196\pardeftab720\li196\fi-197\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	
\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The Ace of trumps and the Ten of trumps (provided it is at least a secondary trump card),\
\ls1\ilvl0\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	
\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone if the suit of the dealer's 20 can be calculated, the suit's ace and at least a secondary ten.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
A player wishing to kontra can expect to take roughly half of the other valuable cards held in their hand. They have a chance of winning the final trick if they hold three or four high trumps and a hand containing all four suits. According to a realistic calculation you can expect 10 points from your partner in the form of a trick, stealing or discarding. If the result calculated in this way exceeds the declarer's score by 10 points, the hand is a candidate for a double; a 30-point difference always justifies doubling. Declarations of 40 and 20 by the defenders can, in themselves, represent an insurmountable advantage in terms of winning the hand.\
One can confidently double the "40-100" contract if holding either the King or the Ober of trumps. Otherwise, a trump suit consisting of A-X-x, A-x-x-x, or X-x-x-x is particularly suitable for doubling\'97provided one is short on major cards\'97as this allows the partner to discard useful cards on the trump tricks. A double can also be based on a weaker trump holding if the opening lead reveals a shared three- or four-card suit with the declarer that guarantees multiple tricks. High cards in the side suits do not contribute much trick-taking power against the "40-100" contract; rather, they are valuable primarily as discard material, given that the declarer often has a singleton or even a void in one or two suits.\
\
One should double the ultimo when holding the trump VII. If the declarer\'92s first lead is presumably or certainly their driving suit, and one holds a total of 7 or 8 cards across the trump and driving suits, doubling is always justified. A player holding 6 such cards\'97with at least 2 being trumps\'97should generally double the ultimo; however, one should refrain from doubling if the cards in both suits are merely of medium strength or if one is void in a suit. Holding 3 trumps and 2 cards in the driving suit can also suffice for a double, provided the player holds a sure trick-taking card in both suits. If the declarer leads a trump, the ultimo can be doubled with three higher trumps or four trumps in total, provided one is not void in a suit. A similar hand is desirable for doubling even if the declarer\'92s first lead is a side suit of uncertain nature.\
\
One should double the 20-100 bid if it can be determined from one's own cards that the declarer lacks the twenty-point bonus or that securing at least two major-suit tricks is certain. One can count on the Ace or a Ten-x (or stronger) combination in trumps, a high card won on the first trick, or\'97if the suit of the twenty can be deduced\'97the Ace or at least a Ten-x combination in that suit. A riskier scenario arises when only ten points are guaranteed, one or two tricks are expected in trumps or another suit, and the question is whether the partner can discard an Ace or a Ten. In such a situation, the leader should double, as their partner cannot make a mistake during the discard\'97since the leader is the last to play to the declarer's lead; however, if the defender has a prospect of a similar trick, they should pass instead.\
\
A classic case for doubling a betli is when the doubler is void in a suit. In that missing suit, the partner can either defeat the contract immediately, or the doubler can discard a card of that suit on the lead, allowing the declarer to be put on lead in that suit. If the declarer does not lead the missing suit, the player should consider whether they will be able to signal their void. According to the transfer lead convention, if a player doubling a betli or rebetli leads a medium card (Ober, Unter, X, or IX) as their first lead, leading Acorns signals a void in Leaves (and vice versa), while leading Bells signals a void in Hearts (and vice versa).\
Other unbalanced distributions\'97such as holding two singletons\'97can also justify doubling a betli. Sometimes the first lead reveals that the declarer holds a six- or seven-card suit; in such cases, doubling a standard betli or rebetli is worth considering even with cards that do not immediately guarantee a defeat, as the declarer might hold two voids. One should only double an open betli if the bid was likely forced (e.g., a poor talon was dealt, but the player could no longer bid a cheaper betli) or if it can be deduced that the declarer cannot hold a flawless hand.\
A successful double is virtually assured when the partner holds no cards in the declarer's first suit, while we hold a card that the declarer is forced to overplay. For example, if the partner discards on the opening lead of the King of Bells, we win the trick with the Ace, leaving the IX of Bells in our hand. Since the declarer could have discarded at most two of the Ober, Unter, and X of Bells, we should lead the IX after doubling. Defeat is also certain if no one wins the first lead of that suit, though only a very inattentive declarer would make such a gross error.\
After the RHO doubles, the LHO should generally double as well, unless they hold medium-to-high cards with a 3-3-2-2 or 4-2-2-2 distribution. \
\
Doubling a durchmars is certainly sound when holding three Aces, or when the first lead of a suit can be won, or when holding a trump stopper against a trump honor (Ace, X-x, K-x-x, O-x-x-x, or stronger combinations). If our hand\'97factoring in the strength indicated by the partner's bidding\'97suggests the declarer has at most 9 sure tricks, it is justifiable to double a durchmars combined with ultimo; since the declarer can only play 8 winning cards, we can afford to guard two suits. The case of a Durchmarsch bid alongside a 20-100 is similar, as the declarer is unlikely to risk the "100" just to avoid losing the final trick (though determined Ulti players might do even that). If the declarer has not bid Ulti or is playing a Durchmarsch in no-trump, we should double if we hold stoppers in two or three suits and can reasonably expect to signal this to our partner. Hands suitable for signaling primarily include multiple stoppers (e.g., A-X, X-K-x, A-K, K-O-x, etc.); combinations one card longer than the stopper itself are also effective (e.g., A-x, X-x-x, or K-x-x). If the Durchmarsch without a trump suit is doubled by the player to the declarer's right (RHO), the player to the declarer's left (LHO) should join in if they hold a stopper. One should double an exposed Durchmarsch only in exceptional circumstances\'97specifically, when holding a hand guaranteed to defeat the contract.\
After a double, the declarer must assume a distribution of cards that allows for the contract to be defeated. They should redouble if the contract\'97or the doubled portion thereof\'97can fail only in very rare cases or not at all, or if they have successfully misled the doubler with their opening lead (e.g., bidding Ulti with four good trumps, four "drivers," and an Ace-Ten in one suit, but\'97as a ruse\'97leading the Ace of the short suit rather than the drivingsuit). Rekontra (redouble) and higher-level bids should be made only in special cases and after careful consideration. If the outcome depends on whether the doubler's partner can play a sufficiently valuable card\'97against a 40\'96100 or 20\'96100 bid\'97and holds the necessary cards, that partner should redouble.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Play\
Basic Principles\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Every player\'92s goal\'97with the exception of betli contracts\'97is to utilize the trick-taking power and point-scoring potential of their cards with maximum efficiency. One must keep track of the cards played in suits critical to the outcome of the hand (such as the trump suit or a side suit where one holds length) and monitor whether the two highest-ranking cards in the other suits have already been played. Players strive to exploit the opportunities provided by the "over-play" rule, particularly to promote or capture the Tens. In ambiguous situations, both the declarer and their opponents choose a line of play that reveals the least information to the opposing side about the cards they hold. The declarer should consider the combinatorial reality that if the two other players hold a combined total of five cards in a specific suit, the distribution is most often 3-2 or 2-3, less frequently 4-1 or 1-4, while the probability of a 5-0 or 0-5 split is negligible. Four missing cards will typically be distributed 3-1 or 1-3 (with about 50% probability) or, somewhat less frequently, 2-2; finding all four cards in a single hand is considered exceptional. A distribution of three cards is typically 2-1 or 1-2, while two cards may be held by either one or both opponents. It should be noted, however, that repeated exchanges of the talon\'97especially when all three players have bid\'97increase the frequency of more extreme distributions.\
The defenders employ every available tactical and conventional method to facilitate their partnership play. If no meaningful assistance can (any longer) be expected from one's partner, there is no need to convey information, as the declarer could also draw inferences from the conventions\'97something that is unnecessary under such circumstances.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Parti\
Declarer's Play\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 If the declarer holds a long suit other than trumps, they should begin play in that suit by aiming to win the final trick\'97possibly using the trump 7.\
In the absence of a long suit, it is advisable to choose a line of play that does not affect the score; for example, leading the Ace from an A-x-(x) combination followed by a small card; the King from X-K-(x); or leading from a headless suit of 2 or 3 cards.\
To ensure capturing the Tens, one should generally avoid leading first from suits like X-x-(x), A-K-(x), or A-O-(x), and should avoid leading a singleton small card for as long as possible\'97unless the intention is to discard the Ten. Holding a singleton Ten, it is also correct to wait, as the card might win a trick if a defender discards the Ace on a trick won by the other defender.\
When following to a trick led by an opponent, play the lowest card, or the highest if part of a sequence or pair\'97unless that card is a Ten or would reveal the suit of a "20" (marriage). With K-x or O-x combinations, it is better to play the higher card; this creates the impression that the suit is exhausted, potentially causing a cautious opponent to lead a small card rather than the Ten after playing the Ace.\
Tricks should generally be won with the lowest possible card\'97though the higher card of a pair or the highest of a sequence should be used\'97but one should seize the first opportunity to win a trick with the Ten in side suits where the Ace is missing. If declarer holds A-X (x) in a suit and a defender leads that suit, it is correct to win the first trick with the ace; playing the ten would\
reveal the location of the other high card, prompting the opponents to switch to a different suit.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Defense\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Defenders generally cover the declarer's leads with low cards, especially reserving the Ace if there is a chance of capturing the Ten; conversely, it is advisable to win the trick immediately with their own Tens.\
There is no rush to win a trick with the trump Ten if the Ace has already\
been played or if at least one other card remains alongside the Ten. The defender who gains the lead first should choose a suit that, at the very least, does not jeopardize the chances of defeating the contract.\
Good attacking suits include A-X-x-(x) or X-K-x-(x). In a suit lacking sequence honors, the defender to declarer\'92s left (LHO) should lead the highest card to try and capture the declarer's Ten; the defender to the declarer\'92s right (RHO), however, should lead a low card, as playing a higher card might force their partner to play the Ten in front of the declarer's Ace. LHO should cover the other defender\'92s low lead with at least a medium-ranking card to prevent\
the declarer from winning a trick with a Ober or Unter. If one defender finds a suit that allows for shortening the declarer's trumps, both members of the defensive pair should continue playing that suit. When playing to a suit the declarer is ruffing, RHO should choose a card\
carefully so that if they have to lead that suit later, their partner is not forced to play a valuable card. For example, holding A-K-O-VIII, they should play one of the honors instead of the 8; otherwise, if they gain the lead, they would have to lead into LHO\'92s Ten or sacrifice the Ace.\
It would be a waste of a high card; it might be discarded later on the other defender's winning trick. A common dilemma for the defender is whether to play the ten when partner leads an ace in a side suit. As a general rule, playing the ten is recommended; if the declarer holds a singleton, it gains a trick, and if they are void, they would likely win both high cards anyway. \
Exceptional cases:\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  taking the trick with the Ace is sufficient in itself to set the contract or reach the 100-point mark;\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  it is suspected or certain that the declarer holds the "20" (King-Ober) in that suit;\
