\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone the final level of doubling was redouble or *hirskontra*, as a void in the suit can be assumed in such cases.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When leading from a suit headed by the ten, LHO may lead the ten (especially after partner has doubled); for RHO, however, this play is advisable only in the absence of other winning options. The first option is preferable because if the declarer wins the trick with a trump, the Ace need not be played; this is not the case in the second scenario. Leading the ten from a suit containing the declarer's 20 marriage is never recommended, because even if the declarer does not hold the Ace, this play promotes the King or the Ober.\
\
With a side suit headed by Ace-Ten, a conventional signal may be used: if the suit is three cards long, the Ten is played first, regardless of who led the suit; from a doubleton or a long suit, however, the Ace is led. This small piece of information can prove crucial in certain situations, helping to prevent the defender's partner from being ruffed.\
From A-x-x-x-x, the Ace should be led first; if neither the partner nor the declarer plays the Ten to the trick, one may attempt to capture the Ten with a trump by playing a low card.\
\
If defender holds a side suit of six or seven cards, they might attempt to have their partner ruff out a missing high card.\
When leading from a three- or four-card suit headed by the Ace, the leader may lead a low card to trap the Ten; if the declarer plays the lower card from an X-K or X-O holding, their Ten becomes unprotected.\
A player who has declared a "20" marriage plays the King first from that suit. Consequently, playing or leading the Queen (or another card) denies holding the "20" in that suit. Thus, if a defender has declared a "20" and\'97for instance\'97plays the King when the declarer leads the Ace, their partner can reasonably assume they held the "20" in that suit, although there is a smaller chance they held a singleton King or an Ace-King combination. After the King, it is correct to play the other cards and finally the Queen; this signals that the suit is exhausted.\
Sometimes a "negative marriage signal" becomes necessary\'97specifically when the declarer has declared one or two "20s" and leads trumps or a suit in which they do not hold a "20." In such cases, playing the King or Queen informs the partner that the declarer cannot hold a marriage in that suit; any "20" held by the defender themselves is signaled by playing the King, as previously described. When a defender switches to a new suit\'97provided there is no need to overtake the lead or play the ten under the ace\'97they should follow this convention: with a doubleton, play the lower card first, followed by the higher one; with a suit of three or more cards, play the higher-ranking card first, followed by the lower one. A typical scenario for applying this convention is when a defender leads a suit headed by the ace. Whether the declarer ruffs the first lead or not, the defender will know upon the second lead whether it is safe to play the suit a third time or if doing so would force their partner to ruff. If the partner plays the seven on the first lead, it is immediately obvious that their holding is shorter than three cards; the same applies when the eight is played, provided the seven is held by the leader or has been played by the declarer.\
Thus, depending on the circumstances, three different methods can be used to signal suit length:\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  the method of the initial lead from a suit headed by the Ace and Ten,\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  leading from a suit containing the Twenty,\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  signaling via the rank of the cards played.\
Opponents usually play their lowest card against the declarer's sure tricks, though they might hold back the Seven from a three- or four-card trump holding to set up a "silent Ultimo." From a trump holding of K-O-x, it is advisable to play the Forty card against the declarer's Ace or Ten; if the partner also follows with a trump, the declarer will likely assume a 2-2 split and potentially base a redouble on this or misplay the final trick.\
One can force out the declarer's trump\'97provided the other defender holds no trumps\'97to ensure the partner can safely discard a major card.\
When discarding on a partner's (expected) winning trick, one should primarily play the Ace from an Ace-Ten combination, unless the declarer is known to hold the Twenty in that suit. Secondarily, one should play the Ten from a suit lacking an Ace; the Ace from a suit lacking a Ten should only be played if the Ten has already been played, if 10 points suffice to win the hand or reach the "hundred" mark, or if it has become clear that the declarer holds no cards in that suit. \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Forty-One Hundred\
Declarer's Play\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 The declarer attempts to score the necessary points using the methods already established during the hand. Any trick that must be conceded in the trump suit or another long suit should be surrendered as early as possible; delaying increases the risk of being forced to discard. For example, the trump suit is distributed as follows:\
		Declarer		X K O IX VIII\
		RHO			VII\
		LHO			A U\
If the lead is the Ober or King of trumps, the defenders secure a 10-point trick and cannot discard into the trump suit later; however, if the declarer leads a side suit and the Right-Hand Opponent (RHO) leads the trump seven  or the Left-Hand Opponent (LHO) the trump unter, a valuable card might later fall to the Ace.\
When playing a hand with poor prospects, it is better to start with the Ace of a short suit rather than an Ace-less long suit; this may well prevent the opponents from doubling.\
From a strong trump suit, the declarer should lead one of the "40" cards (King or Ober); if the RHO has no trumps, they will likely play an Ace or Ten, thereby improving the declarer's chances of success.\
Do not lead a singleton small card early in the play; if the opponents split the suit, you have a better chance of capturing one of the high cards (Ace or Ten) if they are held separately. However, you should lead the singleton small card if you have discarded the Ten into the talon.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Defense\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Defenders should aim to capture valuable cards in accordance with the principles of defending against the contract. They should anticipate taking no more than 2\'963 tricks\'97with the declarer taking the final one\'97yet realize that securing 40 points is enough to defeat the contract.\
It is advisable to cover the declarer's leads with low cards, though one should win immediately with the Ten of a side suit if the Ace is missing. When the declarer leads a trump or a card that can be ruffed, it is correct to play the Seven last, unless doing so would result in losing the trick (such as with a Ten-Seven combination). Since the declarer cannot hold the 7 (otherwise they would have called ultimo), partner is thereby informed that a trump remains in the hand.\
The LHO defender should lead a singleton, non-trump if there is a prospect of scoring a ruff; otherwise, holding a King or Ober in a side suit, they might attempt to lead through the declarer\'92s potential Ten.\
The RHO defender may lead a small singleton trump, but should also lead trump when leading another suit risks running into a tenace (2 high cards of a suit missing the card between them) or promoting the declarer\'92s weak Ten (naturally, leading from a holding like X-x in trumps is only advisable if the Ace is no longer held).\
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Ultim\'f3\
Declarer\'92s Play\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Tactics vary depending on the strength of the hand, but a constant element is the need to keep track of the trumps already played and\'97if applicable\'97the cards played in the "driving suit" (the suit one intends to force out). Novice players often lose the Ultim\'f3 because, unaware of how many trumps remain in play, they wastefully play their trumps during the middle game.\
With six or more trumps, success is easy; play can begin with a trump, or one might start with a low card in a side suit to bait the opponents into doubling (kontra), which can then be redoubled (rekontra) with good odds. If the side suits lack high honors, one should lead with a trump.\
Holding three to five trumps and a driving suit, play should begin with the driving suit. Start with the Ace; if the Ace is missing but the Ten is held, it is advisable to lead the second-highest card; a driving suit lacking high honors should be opened with its highest card. Subsequently, one should continue playing the driving suit until the opponents run out of trumps or can no longer trump in. After the Ace or the second-highest card, the correct next move is to lead the Ten (which has now become the highest remaining card in the suit), unless the Ultim\'f3 can be won securely without risking the Ten.\
In the absence of a driving suit, it is advisable to lead from the trump suit (specifically, when five trumps are held). With a 4-3-2-1 or 4-2-2-2 distribution, play may begin with any side suit other than the singleton; however, one may also lead the singleton if two cards have been discarded from that suit, or one may lead the top trump card (A-X-x-VII). Play should continue by alternating suits and using trump strength efficiently, while ensuring that trump control\'97an Ace or other high card\'97is preserved until the eighth trick. A declarer lacking the King and Ober of trumps can infer the trump distribution based on whether or not a "40" is declared. For example, if the declarer holds five trumps and no "40" is announced, the opponents' trumps are distributed 2-1 or 1-2 (barring the rare instance where a marriage is withheld).\
\
If the last card (the ultimo) is weak and the declarer holds a holding like X-K in a suit, they might play the ten on the opponent's ace; assuming the opponents have only one or two cards in that suit, they will likely lead it again rather than seeking a more dangerous line of attack (such as a void or a short suit). Defenders can sometimes spot this ruse: if the declarer plays the ten on the Left-Hand Opponent's (LHO) ace, the Right-Hand Opponent (RHO) should play the king; if the king is not in the LHO's hand and their partner does not play it either, they must switch suits, as the declarer was almost certainly attempting a deception.\
If the defenders happen to lead into a suit where the declarer holds K-O-x, the declarer should play the intermediate cards (the King or Ober) on the high cards; this way\'97provided the length hasn't been signaled or the leader hasn't noticed it\'97they will continue the suit, allowing one defender to give the other a ruff.\
When the forces are evenly matched, the outcome of the ultimo is decided in the endgame.\
If the declarer holds an unbroken two-card combination without the ace, they should lead the higher of the two cards on the eighth trick\'97for example, in a situation like this:\
			Declarer	RHO		LHO\
	Acorns		     -		   -		    IX\
	Leaves		   VII		   - 		     -\
	Bells		     -		   -		     -\
	Hearts		  X VII	         A O VIII 	   K U\
The declarer is playing a Leaves Ultimo and has just won the seventh trick. If he is reluctant to part with his heart ten and leads the seven instead, RHO covers with a card lower than the Ace, LHO takes the trick, and the Acorn nine forces out the trump seven; however, if the ten is led, RHO is forced to win the trick and can only lead a heart. Of course, the dangerous card might be held by the player with the Ace, but this line of play offers the only hope of saving the Ultimo.\
While prioritizing the successful completion of the Ultimo, the declarer must also pay attention to the game score. In the event of a higher-level double, winning the game itself may become the primary objective.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Defense\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 A key issue is finding the right suit to attack\'97one that forces a ruff from the declarer without shortening the partner's trump holding. Since the declarer's first-bid suit is presumed to be the driving suit (the suit they intend to run), the defender generally has to choose between two suits (or three, in the case of a trump lead or a deceptive opening lead). The decision rests with the defender who wins the first trick, usually at the second or third trick.\
The stronger defender\'97holding a combined total of four high cards or at least five cards across the trump suit and the driving suit\'97should lead from a different long suit (ideally headed by the Ace), which the partner should continue if they gain the lead. If the stronger defender has a choice of two suits to attack and one is a heart, they should choose the other, as the likelihood of the declarer being able to discard hearts cards is lower.\
If the defender who is weaker (or of roughly equal strength to their partner) wins the lead first, they should lead from a two- or three-card suit, preferably one headed by the Ace.\
\
Leading the attack suit should begin with the Ace\'97or the Ten from an Ace-Ten-small holding (following the "Ace against a Ten-holding" principle)\'97for the following reasons:\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  alongside long suits, the declarer often holds a singleton, making it possible to take two tricks;\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  the declarer cannot win a trick with a lone Ten;\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  signaling suit length is easier.\
Partner should correctly play the Ten to the Ace, even if the declarer plays last to the trick; the only exception is when the declarer can be deduced to hold the 20 marriage in that suit.\
If the Ten does not appear on the Ace and the declarer cannot hold the 20 marriage, a new suit must be led, as the declarer evidently holds an established Ten.\
Returning the declarer's first suit is permissible only if it cannot be a suit the declarer is trying to establish\'97specifically, if the first trick revealed it to be weak and at most three cards long (e.g., an opponent holds four higher cards in it, and partner also follows suit).\
Any method of signaling suit length may be used during the defense. If partner is out of the attack suit, continuing with it is permissible only if they have no trumps left or if ruffing is irrelevant to the outcome; otherwise, a new suit should be sought.\
It may happen that the declarer leads trumps and one opponent is void\'97suggesting a 5-3-0 trump split. All this creates a good opportunity for teamwork: the player without trumps can signal their best suit by discarding. Ideally, they should discard from a holding of at least three cards headed by the Ace\'97specifically the lowest card\'97though with a holding like A-X-x-(x-x), playing the Ace itself onto the partner's trick is the correct move. If they hold no Ace-led suit, or only a short one (1\'962 cards), they should play the highest card of their longest suit; however, if that card is the ten and the declarer is winning the trick, it is advisable to play the second-highest card instead. Among suits of equal length, the stronger one should be signaled. Since the player holds no trumps, the most balanced distribution is 4-3-3, meaning the signaled suit will be at least four cards long. Armed with this information, the partner can identify the most effective defensive strategy with greater confidence, and the defender might even base a double on this signal.\
\
It is advisable to call for the trump against the *Ultimo* only in exceptional situations\'97specifically, when the trump card is a guaranteed winner but playing a different suit would require leading into an opponent's strength; furthermore, the master trump should be played on the eighth trick if the declarer holds two trumps and the remaining trump can be drawn out on the ninth trick.\
Let's say one opponent (RHO) has a Leaves Ulti against these cards:\
		Acorns		-\
		Leaves		A X IX\
		Bells		K IX VIII VII\
		Hearts		A O U\
The declarer leads the Ace of Acorns, which the player ruffs with the Nine of Leaves. The best strategy now is to draw the two high trumps and then\'97depending on the partner's first discard\'97attempt to play either Bells or Hearts. Since the Ultimo cannot be captured outright but only forced out, it is advantageous for the declarer to shorten their hand in the second or third trick.\
If a trump is led by declarer, the player holding the Ace and Ten of trumps (RHO) should win the trick with the Ace; this prevents the declarer from immediately realizing that the other high trump is also held by the defense. \
During the opening and middle game (tricks 4\'967), discarding a high card from an unbroken suit is incorrect, as it risks losing a trick and thereby diminishes the chances of defeating the Ultimo. Discarding an Ace that is not accompanied by the Ten should be particularly avoided. In a suit led by the Ace and Ten, one should part with the Ace first\'97signaling the presence of the other high card\'97and play the Ten only when forced to do so or when retaining it is of no consequence.\
Beyond the Ultimo, the defenders also play against the contract itself. They should focus primarily on the Parti (the base contract) if there is no hope of defeating the *Ultimo*\'97or if they have at least Recontra'd\'97making the base contract the more valuable objective.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Twenty-Hundred\
Declarer's Play\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 When a "20-100" is declared, the suit distribution is usually more extreme than average\'97whether due to the initial deal or as a result of multiple exchanges with the talon. Consequently, the declarer must exercise caution when conceding a trick.\
A trick that must be conceded in a short side suit (typically involving an X-K holding) should be surrendered early in the play, while the risk of an opponent discarding a losing card is still low.\
If the Ace or Ten is missing from the "20" suit, it is often better to lead the Ace of the short suit first, then switch to the long suit. In such a scenario, drawing trumps is ill-advised; if the opponents run out of trumps or discard a short suit, it increases the risk that one of them will play the missing Ace or Ten on the other's trick. Losing the very first trick must be avoided at all costs, as the opponents will almost certainly double the contract in that event.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Defense\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Above all, opportunities to discard must be utilized. If the defender holds at most two high cards, they must carefully consider on which trick to discard them, given that defenders playing against a "20-100" usually win only one or two tricks. Opportunities to discard can sometimes be created by drawing out a singleton or the last trump, or\'97if void in the trump suit\'97by ruffing or discarding to shed a different short suit. Here, too, we hold back the trump VII\'97within the limits of possibility\'97so that partner can infer the trump distribution.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Betli\
The Declarer's Play\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Naturally, play should begin with the worst card. If the declarer is not forced to overplay or can discard a different suit, they should get rid of their most dangerous card; however, among adjacent cards, they should discard the lower one\'97for example, playing the nine (from a sequence of Ten-Nine) against an Ace or another suit. If an opponent leads a suit in which the declarer holds a sequence of at least three cards starting with the Seven, it is advisable to play the Seven or the Eight, as the opponents may not dare to continue that suit.\
In certain situations, playing a \'93strong\'94 card can create the impression that the declarer has discarded their \'93weak\'94 cards into the talon, causing opponents to avoid leading the suit that poses a threat to them. For example, suppose the declarer holds the Unter, Ten, and Seven of an\
unbroken suit; the Right Hand Opponent (RHO) leads the Ace, and the Left Hand\
Opponent (LHO) discards. If the declarer now plays the Unter or the Ten,\
the opponents will almost certainly follow up with the Eight or the Nine\'97if only to allow the LO to discard again; however, if the declarer plays the Seven, the caller will likely assume the declarer has discarded the two higher cards and will try a different suit instead. By then, the opponents may no longer be able to regain the lead, or the declarer might have already managed to get rid of the two weak cards.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Defensive Play\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 When the RHO has a choice, they decide whether to cover the initial lead with a low or high card based on whether they hold a card likely to defeat the contract\'97such as a Seven in a suit where they lack the Eight. If you hold a sequence in the lead card's suit starting with the Ace (e.g., A, K, O...), play the lowest card; this signals to your partner that the higher cards are either in your hand or in the talon.\
Against a non-doubled contract, the player to the leader's left (LHO) may lead a 7 or 8 of a specific suit with the aim of immediately defeating the contract. If a defender leads a high card and then switches suits, it usually indicates that the high card was a singleton. A player holding a five-card suit that includes the 7 should call for the high card first, followed by the 7.\
The player doubling the betli usually holds a void in a suit, a fact that is crucial for their partner to know. In the simpler scenario, the doubler's void is revealed on the very first trick; naturally, their partner then leads a low card of that suit\'97or, lacking one, forces the declarer to discard a higher card. If the declarer does not lead the suit in which the doubler is void, the doubler attempts to win the first trick (i.e., if sitting to the declarer's right, they cover with a high card). If the opponent who did not double (or did not double first) wins the trick, they must urgently shift play to their partner using a suit of medium length; the partner then signals the void suit by requesting a specific card (e.g., leading a medium-value Acorn card indicates a void in Leaves, and vice versa; leading a Bell card indicates a void in Hearts, and vice versa).\
Subsequent leads by the doubler do not serve this signaling purpose, nor can the LHO leads be considered a transfer signal if both players have doubled. If the doubler is not void in any suit or has no opportunity to signal, they should attempt other defensive strategies against the betli.\
If the declarer discards a card of a different suit in response to a lead, playing that suit should generally be stopped immediately. Exceptionally, however, it may be worth continuing with a suit that both the declarer and the partner discard on (a double discard) if the player leading holds a low card and no other opportunity to defeat the contract presents itself. No special strategy is required to defeat a open betli; once the hands are revealed, it is usually immediately apparent whether the contract can be defeated. Playing out the hand is generally only worthwhile if the defenders attempt to succeed by employing a double-discard tactic.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Durchmars\
Declarer's Play\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 When holding trumps, the declarer must first draw the opponents' trumps. Once the defenders are out of trumps\'97or if the durchmars is being played without trumps and there are still tricks to be won\'97the winning cards should be called for in a random order so that the suit of the losing card cannot be inferred. While playing out 8 or 9 sure tricks, the opponents might discard cards that could have won a trick, or the distribution of the declarer's missing cards might prove favorable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Defense\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 In a durchmars that can be defeated, success depends primarily on the defenders' cooperation. If the declarer plays a trump, they are certainly missing one of the "40" cards (King or Ober) and the trump Seven (otherwise they would have bid it); therefore, when the high trumps are played, defenders should first discard any other cards they hold to inform each other about the number of trumps the declarer possesses\'97for example, playing the Seven from an Ace-Seven pair is the correct move. A crucial aspect of the defense is mutually signaling which suits are being guarded, ensuring that both players do not hold onto the same suit. These holdings should be signaled as early as possible, during the initial discards.\
Defending signals against a Durchmars:\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  From a suit headed by A-10, discard the Ace;\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  From a suit headed by 10-K-x (or longer), discard the 10;\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  From a suit headed by A-x, 10-x-x, K-x-x-x (or longer), discard the lowest card.\
Defending against a Durchmars (no trump):\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  From a suit headed by A-K, discard the Ace;\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  From a suit headed by K-O-x (or longer), discard the King;\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  From a suit headed by A-x, K-x-x, Q-x-x-x (or longer), discard the lowest card.\
Thus, if the partner's first discard is a medium-sized card, they likely do not hold a stopper, although it is possible they hold a combination that cannot be signaled (a singleton Ace, 10-x, or K-x, etc.), or the signal is unclear because the lowest card is the 9 or higher (though discarding the 9 is unambiguous if the partner holds the 7 and 8).\
A player holding a three-card stopper\'97A-10-K or A-K-O\'97 may discard their second-highest card after the signal, since only one trick is needed to defeat the Durchmars.\
The signaled stopper must be retained until the end of the play, unless it becomes apparent that the declarer holds no winning cards in that suit.\
If your partner has signaled a catch, and after showing any catch of your own, you must discard cards in the suit your partner indicated\'97playing from the highest card down; however, you should only discard the Ace if your partner signaled holding the 10-K-x or K-O-x. Next, rid yourself of suit fragments that have little or no chance of winning a trick. Watch the other defender's play, as important inferences can be drawn from it. For example, in a durchmars without a trump suit, if the declarer wins six consecutive tricks in a long suit and a defender holds O-U-9-8 in another suit, that defender should play the 8 as a signal on their first discard. Handling the suit depends on the partner's play as follows:\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  if they do not discard from that suit, the O-U-9 must be kept;\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  if the King is discarded, keeping any two of the cards is sufficient;\

\f2 \uc0\u9679 
\f1  if they play the 10 and the 7, all cards in that suit should be discarded, as none of them can win a trick.\
Whether the declared durchmars succeeds or fails is no longer in doubt after the first trick; actual play continues only if the durchmars fails but the outcome of a smaller bid made alongside it has not yet been decided.\
