\f0\b \cf2 "Silent" Plays\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Achieving a "silent 100" requires essentially the same gameplay as winning a standard game; however, to succeed, it is worth trying riskier strategies than usual\'97for instance, the declarer might attempt to trap the trump ten, or a defender might lead the ten of a side suit (when holding the king and ober but not the ace).\
The declarer might aim for a "silent Ultimo" if they hold two weak four-card suits, five trumps in a weak hand, or perhaps a three-card suit and a strong lead. A defender might consider a silent Ultimo if holding 3\'964 trumps and a strong long suit. A player planning a silent play should try to force the other two players to ruff and keep track of the trumps already played. It can happen that the declarer and one defender exhaust each other's trumps, allowing the other defender to potentially complete an Ultimo with just one or two trumps remaining. By the seventh or eighth trick, one must decide whether to attempt the silent play; if not, the trump seven should be played to avoid being left holding it\'97where it might be forced out by the "over-trump" rule or a suit-following requirement\'97and subsequently having to pay the penalty for a failed silent Ultimo. However, one can safely retain the trump 8\'967 (or 9\'967, etc., if the 8 is missing) a bit longer, as the 7 can still be successfully played from this combination even on the ninth trick.}